# 0034 - the table's word removes a member; a founder's kick counts only with it.
#
# D-045 (2026-09-12), the owner's rule: no peer may stay in a table's group
# as a lingering member once it no longer plays -- the playing peers remove it, on their
# own certifications, and even the founder of a table of three or more must
# not be able to remove a legitimate peer of the group on its own.
#
# The library's group has one admin, the founder, whose kick every member
# honours on its signature alone. This patch keeps two lists on a chat, by
# group encryption key, that the application fills from the table's word --
# the players' certificate of a seat that has fallen silent, or the roster
# the founder said again before the first hand:
#
#   tox_group_peer_drop(tox, group, key, for_good): the member is dropped from
#   this client's view of the group as a kicked one (the saved-peer entry goes
#   with it, patch 0031), and with for_good the key is refused for the chat's
#   life -- peer_add returns -3 for it, whatever brings it back.
#
#   tox_group_peer_allow_kick(tox, group, key): the founder's kick of this key
#   is honoured here. In handle_gc_kick_peer a kick of any key not allowed is
#   ignored -- by every member, the target included -- and logged.
#
# Every member acts on the word it verified itself, so a member that no longer plays is gone from
# every view whether or not the founder lives; a founder that kicks without
# the word changes nothing but its own view. The wire is untouched: no new
# packet, no new field, and a client of the public network in a group of its
# own is unaffected -- our groups are private and hold only our clients.
#
# Applied to the vendored tree in place; this file is the record. The marker
# tools/build-tox.ps1 checks for is the comment in tox.h.
diff --git a/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/group_chats.c b/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/group_chats.c
